dvanced in  insanity defense and how that might  affect someone's ability to confess so  what what the defense will be trying to  do is demonstrate that that  confession obtained isn't as credible as  it might otherwise be so you put a false  confession expert up on the stand to  talk about to raise doubt to raise that  reasonable doubt regarding the veracity  of the confession along with all the  other evidence to show that he really  didn't properly confess but that's what  this the defense is going to be trying  to do here to cast doubt on everything  the state is advancing forward because  of his mental state yeah as i said again  make sure to check the long crime  network for gavel to gavel coverage on  that trial and ohio testimony wrapped up  for the week on thursday in the case of  accused killer william hussell the  former icu physician is on trial for  allegedly killing patients with  excessive amounts of fentanyl the 14  deaths he's charged with happened  between 2015 and 2018. who sells defense  team argues the former doctor was giving  end-of-life comfort care to the patients  he was treating whoso faces 15 years to  life on each count if convicted over the  last five weeks the state's case has  focused on the amounts of medicine whose  cell ordered nursing staff to administer  to patients jurors have heard from  husel's former co-workers at mount  carmel hospital and from family members  of the several of the alleged victims  thursday's testimony included the  daughter of melissa pennix the woman  whose death reportedly sparked the  initial investigation into whoso  i said  if  there's no hope why were you  there's nothing you can do if there's no  hope why was something being put in our  arm when we walked in  and he said oh we never stop trying  what happened after uh the discussion  about  withdrawing care  well we talked about it as a family and  considering that we aren't none of us  are medical experts and if a doctor  tells you  that your loved 